    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. For q-129423, zeroing a22, a33, and a44 leaves leading entries in columns 2 and 3, followed by two zero rows; every other offered replacement violates row echelon form. For q-37266, B={3,6,9,12} and C={6,12,18} contain only positive multiples of 3, while D contains 2. For q-37265, only the offered B={2,6,12,14,32} consists entirely of positive even integers. For q-1191 and q-80809, independently including or excluding each element gives respectively eight and four subsets, including the empty set and the full set. For q-1208, C⊆Z is false because 2i is complex but not an integer; the other inclusions hold. For q-105033, negative integers are non-positive rational numbers, but Z and N contain 1. Back substitution gives q-10196: (0,2,3); q-129455: (2/3,1,-1/3); q-20452: (0,4,-3); q-129457: (5,-5/3,2); q-129615: (2,-1,2); and q-129604: (-1,3,-1). Each triple satisfies its original equations. For q-174342, substituting i=0 gives {-1,0,1,2}. For q-172665, substituting n=4 gives {1,2,3,4,5}. For q-174434, (0,1) is contained in (0,2), and adjoining {0} produces [0,2).",
